 lake I actually saw  Claire nice standing in front of roughly  about sixteen feet and they are really  well the website and directions said  give yourself fifteen to twenty minutes  to put them together and so Claire and I  are like cotton that's great we can do  that so we put it off until the last  thing we did it took us a little bit  longer than 15 minutes it took us about  two and a half three and a half hours  they are a little bit more complicated  than we initially thought  so you can see a getting darker and  darker the other thing that we kind of  put off because we didn't know the  carrying capacity was the grocery list  and knowing that we were going to be  kayaking in multiple miles a day and I  put together a last minute but very  calorie efficient grocery list and with  what was available at the Panamanian  grocery store on island of del Toro  cheese peanut butter apples water rum  calorie efficient so Instagram became  this wave to tell these little vignettes  these stories and or as I like to call  them learning curves of what was  actually happening on that expedition so  learning curve number one Claire and I  are in a rush to get out the door start  packing we finally figure out that  everything fits in the both wins both  float whenever cute we finally start  paddling and it's been a year and a half  since I've kayak I was carrying 110  liters through the temperate rainforest  in Canada I had not been ruined still a  few miles into the trip I am hoping that  my arms fall off to give me an excuse to  stop paddling against the current and I  start to kind of panic because the  current is really really strong on the  ocean side and so it's pulling me one  way and I'm just digging with my left  hand and I'm starting to panic and  Claire's a little bit away from me and  so I paddle in and I yell Claire how  much further do we have to go and she's  paddling digging with her left paddle  she's like I don't know look at the GPS  I'm paddling and like you have the GPS  and we both kind of turn and look at  each other and stop paddling and realize  that we left the GPS and yes this is a  circumnavigation there's an island on  one side there's an ocean on the other  it can't be that hard right well there  are these mangrove entrances with GPS  coordinates the mangroves are so thick  from the outside and a lot of places  that we were going to stay we actually  could not locate where the mangrove  entrances were and this is when we  finally found one on the first night we  were going to fishing lodge and they  just happened to be blowing through  conch shells and you're like let's  follow the conch shells that sounds  promising and we ended up there so good  so this is the Instagram post that we  did with this the best and scariest  parts about expeditions are the unknown  they were quite a handful on this trip  Clare Fiedler tending to the fire after  a long day of open ocean dealing with my  seasickness and the last GPS learning  curve number two because we were lost we  were supposed to sleep at an indigenous  village and both of us were so excited  about this because the southern part of  the island is not a tourist destination  it does not get visited very often and  we had the opportunity to camp with the  indigenous people in their village which  was so exciting as because we were lost  we couldn't find the mangrove entrance  we decided to camp on this beach instead  at last photo that you saw that's where  we camped and we decided the next  morning that's okay we will find this  village we will portage which when full  of possessions the kayaks so when we  lifted them up Sportage  started to bow the other way under the  weight which is not something you want  from your foldable kayak so scratch that  plan dump out all of our possessions  refold the boats start portaging the  boats with our camera gear on our backs  and we start to go down this trail which  it starts on this bridge and then the  trail is off to my right and we start to  go down this bridge and we run into a  local so exciting and the local gives us  some very very key information Clara  Fiedler in the short portage originally  Clare and I were going to portage our  gear for a mile to an indigenous village  ready for packing up start hiking we  were told that the dense jungle path we  were going to use was actually a  crocodile trail narrowly avoided that  one so Clara and I had come up with a  hashtag paddling Panama kind of see it  down there on the left and and we use  that to curate these images together to  do this eventual blog post - here on my  left and Claire's house circumnavigated  an island by kayak is like speed dating  and we we were laughing and joking while  we were paddling that expeditions are  like Explorer  matcom because you're trying to put out  these small little fires when you're  panicking and you're like how do you  deal with it like that means you okay  we're good we can go into the field  together or match so Instagram has been  so much for my life and it's really  broadened my world it's allowed me to  connect with people that I that I never  dreamed of connecting with and share my  images just outside of my community and  I will continue to share it's also  introduced me to some of my best trim my  best friend Heidi and these are seven  year old chickens sitting on our heads  it's introduced me to some wonderful  people and it's not as it's broad in my  world it's really it's really shrunk it  to I use it to check conditions of  places if if I'm going somewhere and  need to look up the weather then I will  look up the location and and see what  has briefly been posted I obviously use  it for business and but yeah also shrunk  my world and introduced me to some  really wonderful people it's such a  great tool for connecting so I hope you  will connect me there too  [Applause]
 

